\section*{Verification note}

Each row was checked by exact (arbitrary-precision) substitution. Both signs
\(y=\pm|y|\) are valid since the equation contains only \(y^{2}\).

\section*{Method and completeness}

Write \(A=36n^{3}-19-12mn\); the equation reads \(A^{2}-y^{2}=8m^{3}\).
With \(A=d+e\), \(y=d-e\) this is \(de=2m^{3}\), so every solution with given
\(m\neq 0\) arises from a signed divisor pair of \(2m^{3}\): the pair
determines \(A\), and \(n\) must be an integer root of
\begin{equation*}
36n^{3}-12mn-(A+19)=0,
\end{equation*}
forcing \(A\equiv 5\pmod{12}\). Enumerating all divisor pairs of \(2m^{3}\)
for all \(m\) in a range (segmented-sieve factorization; pairs \((t,s)\)
generated multiplicatively in tandem; exact \(128\)-bit root tests) finds
\emph{all} integer solutions with \(|m|\) in that range and \(n\) unbounded.

\begin{proposition}
Table~\ref{tab:quartic_integer_solutions} contains every nontrivial integer
solution with \(1\le|m|\le 2.5\times10^{8}\); these are the \(37\) rows other
than rows \(29\) and \(35\), whose \(|m|\) values
(\(5.38\times10^{12}\) and \(5.26\times10^{8}\)) lie outside the exhaustively
searched window. The computational cost of extending the window is
\(\sum_{|m|\le M}\tau(2m^{3})\), which is approximately \(9\times10^{11}\)
divisor pairs for \(M=10^{9}\) (about five single-core CPU-hours with the
accompanying program; the work parallelizes trivially over \(m\)-ranges).
\end{proposition}

\section*{Mordell--Weil data for small fibers}

For fixed \(n\), with \(X=-2m\), \(Y=y\), \(c=36n^{3}-19\), the equation is
the elliptic curve \(E_{n}\colon Y^{2}=X^{3}+36n^{2}X^{2}+12ncX+c^{2}\).
Two-descent (\texttt{ellrank}, PARI/GP 2.15.4) gives exact ranks
\begin{equation*}
\operatorname{rk}E_{1}=\operatorname{rk}E_{15}=\operatorname{rk}E_{114}
=\operatorname{rk}E_{131}=1,\qquad
\operatorname{rk}E_{-4}=\operatorname{rk}E_{46}=\operatorname{rk}E_{-54}
=\operatorname{rk}E_{-57}=\operatorname{rk}E_{75}=2,\qquad
\operatorname{rk}E_{19}=3,
\end{equation*}
with \(E_{19}\)-generators of \(X\)-coordinate \(2394\), \(7703436/1369\),
\(2123383/2601\); for \(E_{-29}\) the rank lies in \([1,3]\) and for
\(E_{93}\) in \([1,2]\).

\begin{remark}
Enumerating \(\mathbb{Z}\)-linear combinations of the computed generators
(coefficients to \(\pm14\), \(\pm7\), \(\pm4\) by rank, plus torsion) produced
no integral even-\(X\) points beyond the table: the listed points exhaust the
integral points of small combination index on these fibers.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The ten new rows extend the known range of \(n\) from \([-1641,5118]\) to
\([-9925,27949]\). Five of the ten have \(|n|>9000\), all found with
\(|m|<2.3\times10^{8}\): integral points with large \(|n|\) and moderate
\(|m|\) appear systematically invisible to fixed-\(n\) searches, while the
divisor-pair method captures them uniformly.
\end{remark}

